  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1214 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will continue to influence our weather \nthrough the long term, but will remain centered west of the local \narea. Meanwhile, mean troughing will be present over the Great \nLakes region and into the remainder of E CONUS. This results in a \nperiod of northwest flow aloft with several shortwaves embedded in\nthe flow passing overhead. Thus, while heat will remain a \nconcern, periodic shower and storm chances are also expected over \nthe coming days as the weak frontal boundary remains in the \nvicinity.\n\nShowers and a thunderstorm or two may be ongoing Friday morning, \nbut with a weakening trend expected. By the afternoon hours, \nredevelopment will be possible across SE OK and into W-Central AR.\nLocally gusty winds will remain the primary hazard. A wave will \nthen move through the region Friday night into Saturday morning, \npotentially resulting in additional showers and thunderstorms \novernight, particularly over NE OK and NW AR. At least low precip \nchances continue into the weekend as additional disturbances and \nstronger flow move over the CWA. Heights begin to decrease \nslightly early to mid next week. This may eventually result in \nbetter rain chances, but we will have to wait and see. Overall, \nrainfall continues to look rather spotty and unimpressive for the \nnext several days...though any is better than nothing since \nmoderate to severe drought has now taken hold in our area.\n\nTemperatures will remain above average through the forecast \nperiod, with most locations in the upper 90s or lower 100s over \nthe next several days. A reinforcing weak front may provide \nslightly cooler conditions Sunday across NE OK and NW AR (mid \n90s), but otherwise, heat indices will continue to end up near or \ngreater than 105 and another Heat Advisory has been issued for \nFriday afternoon across much of E OK and W-Central AR. Depending \non dew point trends, the advisory may need to be expanded north in\nfuture forecast updates. Additional heat headlines are likely for\nat least parts of the area over the coming days. With continued \nhot and dry conditions, limited fire weather concerns will \npersist, especially in areas which miss out on any rainfall.",
